    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Ventilator dependence with diaphragm pacing (Z99.11)</content>. Steady progress: two and a half hours twice a day with good saturation. [S]<list><item>Continue the weekly step-up as tolerated.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic incomplete C2 tetraplegia (G82.52)</content>. 25-hydroxyvitamin D 34 ng/mL after eight weeks of vitamin D3: replete. Skin intact; no autonomic dysreflexia. The seating review advised at the consultation has not happened. [S]<list><item>Continue vitamin D3 1977 units daily; recheck yearly.</item><item>Clinic to call the seating service today for the first available appointment, with another provider if it cannot be offered within a month.</item><item>Nurses to continue skin checks morning and night.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Autonomic dysreflexia: episodes, baseline blood pressure, written plan, and nurse training</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No episodes</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Skin check and Braden scale</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Intact; Braden 11</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Seating, cushion, and pressure mapping</td><td>At least yearly and after any change in equipment, weight or skin</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Missed: pressure mapping advised at the consultation has not been done because the seating clinic cancelled for equipment repair. No harm so far: skin intact, Braden unchanged, no lasting redness. Corrective action: the clinic called the seating service today and will use another provider if no appointment is offered within a month; nurses continue twice-daily skin checks</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Catheter care and urinary symptoms</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Changed on schedule; no symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Bowel program</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Effective</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Pacer conditioning, oxygen saturation, and secretion clearance</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Pacer two and a half hours twice a day; saturation 98% on the pacer today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Backup ventilator, suction, cough-assist device, and power plan</td><td>Every visit, with monthly checks by his nurses</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Monthly checks done; travel kit complete</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>25-hydroxyvitamin D</td><td>At baseline, 8 to 12 weeks after repletion, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>34 ng/mL after repletion</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain score and pain plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Pain score 2</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Venous thromboembolism: leg swelling</td><td>Every visit; prophylaxis only during acute illness, surgery or immobilization</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No leg swelling</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Lengthen time on the diaphragm pacer to at least three hours twice a day by six months, with stable oxygen saturation. In progress; target 2003-10-26 [S]</item><item>No pressure injury: skin intact at every check through six months. In progress; target 2003-10-26 [S]</item><item>Written autonomic dysreflexia plan held by him and every nurse, with all nurses trained, by the two-week visit. Outcome by 2003-04-27: Plan and wallet card in place; all regular nurses trained [S]</item><item>25-hydroxyvitamin D at least 30 ng/mL within three months. Outcome by 2003-06-22: 34 ng/mL at ten weeks [S]</item><item>No unplanned hospital admission through six months. In progress; target 2003-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evaluation and Management of Autonomic Dysreflexia and Other Autonomic Dysfunctions: Preventing the Highs and Lows (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2021); Pressure Ulcer Prevention and Treatment Following Spinal Cord Injury: A Clinical Practice Guideline for Health-Care Providers, Second Edition (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2014); Bladder management for adults with spinal cord injury: a clinical practice guideline for health-care providers (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2006); The AUA/SUFU Guideline on Adult Neurogenic Lower Urinary Tract Dysfunction: Treatment and Follow-up (2021); Management of Neurogenic Bowel Dysfunction in Adults after Spinal Cord Injury: Clinical Practice Guideline for Health Care Providers (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2021); Respiratory management following spinal cord injury: a clinical practice guideline for health-care professionals (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2005); Bone Health and Osteoporosis Management in Individuals with Spinal Cord Injury: Clinical Practice Guideline for Health Care Providers (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2022); Prevention of Venous Thromboembolism in Individuals with Spinal Cord Injury: Clinical Practice Guidelines for Health Care Providers, 3rd ed. (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2016); Evaluation and Management of Pain After Spinal Cord Injury (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2026); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, osteoporosis monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in two months with kidney function, the PHQ-9 and the seating report. Next scheduled visit 2003-08-24: four-month review: kidney function, skin, mood, and equipment. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
